    "case_id": "20",
    "case_summary": "A 60-year-old woman presented with a painful nodule in the left lobe of the thyroid. She had no history of head and neck radiation and no family history of thyroid or parathyroid conditions. The nodule had not significantly changed in size, but the pain persisted and was exacerbated by talking. She did not experience any changes in her voice. Five years prior, she had been evaluated for pain radiating over the left side of her face, at which time a small, firm nodule in the left thyroid lobe was detected. A needle biopsy at that time showed no signs of malignancy, and the condition was identified as a benign nodular goiter, with observation recommended. An ultrasound revealed a mass in the left lower thyroid lobe measuring 2.7 × 1.6 × 2.7 cm, containing both cystic and solid components. Her serum ionized calcium level was within normal limits, but her intact parathyroid hormone (PTH) level was elevated to three times the upper limit of normal. Due to ongoing facial pain, a left thyroid lobectomy and isthmusectomy were performed. During surgery, a firm, multinodular left thyroid lobe was observed. No abnormal central compartment lymph nodes were identified or removed.\n\nPathological examination of the specimen revealed a large, yellowish tumor measuring 2.7 × 2.0 × 2.0 cm that was pushing into the left thyroid lobe, but separated from it by a fibrous capsule. The mass was noted to be composed of monomorphous parathyroid cells without significant necrosis or mitotic activity. There was invasion into the thyroid capsule and into adjacent fatty tissue. No vascular invasion was noted. Further immunohistochemical studies found the tumour did not express: Thyroid Transcription Factor – 1, Calcitonin, and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA), but did express Chromagranin, Synaptophysin, Bcl -2 and Cytokeratin 7.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
